+# Phase 2 Audit -- Engagement Solver & Melee (+ demo session-on fixes)
+
+| | |
+|---|---|
+| **Date** | 2026-10-28 |
+| **Auditor** | Senior-dev audit pass (independent of the implementation run) |
+| **Subject** | HEAD `5e46a1b` ("Formation fix") -- the phase-2 engagement-solver run, the movement-cleanup run's landed code, and the demo-fix commits that followed |
+| **Method** | Full read of the combat pipeline (`BattleEngagement` / `BattleMelee` / the six combat processors / registry combat state), the demo module, and the test tier. Static review only: no engine build available in this environment; compile-sanity by symbol/reference check, behavior-safety argued per change. |
+| **Deliverables** | (a) this findings register; (b) one commit per fix group on branch `audit/phase2-engagement` + `.patch` files under `Patches/`; (c) PR writeups in `Patches/PRs/` for everything that needs UE/MCP access. |
+
+---
+
+## 0. Verdict
+
+The architecture held: one system produces formed clashes and mobs, the seams are pure, constants are
+golden-locked, and the flags-off identity discipline is real -- including the worker-thread cvar
+contract, which was verified correct at every call site (see the retracted A-1). The rot is at the
+edges, and it is the kind that compounds: **debug leftovers shipped in the combat path**, **magic numbers re-typed instead
+of shared** (including ones that silently disagree with their neighbors), **fixture data (unit
+composition, keybinds, threshold geometry) hard-coded in C++**, and **unbounded function-local state
+in the locomotion hot path**.
+
+Nothing here requires redesign. Every fix is a deletion, a named constant, or moving data to where
+data lives. The test tier is largely exemplary; the redundancy found is at skeleton level (a
+permanently-failing placeholder that was superseded by a shipped gate) rather than duplication of
+coverage.
+
+---
+
+## 1. Findings register
+
+Severity: **A** = correctness (fix now) · **B** = maintainability/data-drift (fix now, cheap) ·
+**C** = cleanliness (fix now, trivial) · **D** = deferred (needs owner/MCP/engine, PR writeup filed).
+
+### A-tier (correctness)
+
+| ID | Sev | File(s) | Finding |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| **A-1** | ~~High~~ **RETRACTED** | ChargeProcessor / CombatResolutionProcessor | **Retracted during verification.** The first audit pass misread a grep listing and recorded the two worker processors as reading their gates via `GetValueOnGameThread()`. Verified against HEAD: both use `GetValueOnAnyThread()` (`ChargeProcessor.cpp:53`, `CombatResolutionProcessor.cpp:47`), and the `FGarrisonCVars` accessors are correctly partitioned too (the two worker-called ones, `MaxSlotDisplacement`/`MaxZRate`, use AnyThread; the game-thread-only ones use game-thread reads). Kept in this register as a verification record so the next reader doesn't re-chase it. |
+| **A-2** | Med | `UnitLocomotionProcessor.cpp:958`, `:1043` | **Function-local `static TMap` accumulates forever**: `ProbeTicks` (contact-probe tick counters) and `PaceThrottled` (throttle-state latches) are per-process maps keyed by `FUnitHandle`. Stale handles (slot reuse bumps serial, but the map keeps every generation ever seen) grow unbounded across a long session/soak, and state from a dead unit can leak into a new tenant's first probe window ("first 3 ticks" logic reads a warm counter). The handles are unique per generation, so there is no aliasing bug -- only unbounded growth and cross-scenario leakage in the same process. Minimal in-place fix (patched): cull entries whose handle is no longer registered, on a cheap cadence. The clean home (registry state, reset by `ResetBattleState`) is PR-4. |
+| **A-3** | Med | `BattleMelee.cpp:93-99` vs `CombatResolutionProcessor.cpp:124-147` | **Dead seam function `DrainCompositePool`** -- the resolution processor implements the mount-first drain inline (`if (DefenderMountRemaining > 0) --...else AddTag<FDeadTag>`) and never calls the seam's `FMeleeMath::DrainCompositePool`. The golden test `FHitChanceGolden` dutifully locks the seam's behavior, so the project carries a tested function that no runtime path uses. Either the processor should call the seam (behavior change -- needs the golden reviewed) or the seam function + its golden block + its doc-comment should be deleted. This audit deletes the dead code: the inline path is what T2.6/ChargeMechanics verified end-to-end, and the seam's float-based signature (`RiderHP`, `Damage`) doesn't even match the shipped integer-hit model. |
+| **A-4** | Low | `AssignPairsProcessor.cpp:480/511/538` | **O(n) linear scans where the seam already has O(log n)**: the write-back re-finds the pair target / ring defender / stat mirror by scanning the whole `Defenders` array per seeker (three separate scans). The seam sorts `Defenders` handle-wise precisely so `FindDefenderSorted` can binary-search (`BattleEngagement.cpp:247`) -- the processor just doesn't use it (it's file-local to `BattleEngagement.cpp`). At the 2x2400 target this is the difference between 2400*log(n) and ~3*2400*n per tick on the game thread. Fix: expose `FindDefenderSorted` as `FCombatSolver::FindDefender` and use it in the write-back. Non-functional (identical results), so safe without a re-run of the golden tier. |
+
+### B-tier (magic numbers / data-in-code / duplication)
+
+| ID | Sev | File(s) | Finding |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| **B-1** | High | `EngagementClustersProcessor.cpp:89-91` vs `BattleEngagement.h:200-201` vs `AssignPairsProcessor.cpp:507` | **The 40 cm body radius is typed by hand in three places, and two of them disagree with the context they sit in.** (1) `EngagementClustersProcessor` declares local `constexpr BodyRadiusM = 0.4f` and `MaxReachM = 0.7f`, then dilates OBBs by `(2*0.4 + 0.7) * 0.5`; (2) the seam's `FAssignContext` already carries `DefenderRadiusM`/`AttackerRadiusM = 0.4f` plus a reach table where `Normal = 0.7f` -- but the cluster pass never reads it; (3) the assign write-back re-derives the ring radius as the literal `0.4f + 0.4f` instead of `Context.DefenderRadiusM + Context.AttackerRadiusM`, bypassing the `RingRadiusFor` helper the seam itself uses one screen earlier. Fix: give `FCombatSolver` one named constant (`Contact::BodyRadiusM`) + one `RingRadiusFor(reach, context)`; all three sites consume it. The cluster dilation then reads the same values the pairing ring uses -- today a cvar edit to the context defaults would silently desync contact detection from pairing. |
+| **B-2** | Med | `CombatResolutionProcessor.cpp:107-109` vs `BattleMelee.cpp:64-71` | **Knuth multiplicative hash constants duplicated across the seam boundary**: `2654435761u` (golden ratio) appears in both the resolution processor's inline roll hash and the seam's `StaggeredPhaseOffset`, with a second multiplier (`40503u`, `2685821657u`) living only in the processor. Neither site names the other; a future edit to one silently breaks determinism docs (T2.8's "seed-derived" argument) while the golden still passes, because the golden only pins the seam. Fix: move the roll hash into `FMeleeMath` (`CombatRollHash(Entity, RollCount)`) beside the stagger hash; the processor calls it. Pure refactor -- same bits out. |
+| **B-3** | Med | `BattleExecCommands.cpp:770-798` (`SpawnClash`) | **Game data hard-coded in the fixture command**: unit composition (EDU row *indices* 0/1, counts 40/12/8, positions `(0,0)`, `(-24,0)`, `(0,-3)`..., facings 180/0, ranks 3) lives as typed literals in C++. Row indices are the worst part: `SpawnClashUnitExec(World, 1, ...)` means "Sergeant Spearmen" only while the golden EDU keeps its current row order -- the file's own header says values are placeholders pending the Phase-6 audit. Anyone re-ordering the EDU silently turns the knights-vs-peasants mob into peasants-vs-peasants. Fix (patched): drive the variants from a small `static const` table keyed by EDU `dictionary` name via the existing `FindRowByDictionary`-style lookup, so the fixture survives EDU re-ordering; the layout numbers stay in the table (they ARE the scenario, and scenario shape is legitimate fixture data). |
+| **B-4** | Med | `DemoPlayerController.cpp:60-110` | **Keybinds and input geometry hard-coded in C++**: the IMC mappings (LMB/RMB/H/W/A/S/D/Q/E + arrows), click/drag/pick thresholds (5/12/250 px) and the drag segment gap are typed literals in the controller. Per AGENTS §3 (2026-10-25 owner decision) the demo module exists for editor-authored demo UX -- this is exactly the data that belongs in an `UInputMappingContext` asset / `UDataAsset` the editor can own, and it's the same class of issue as B-3. The pure-C++ programmatic-IMC approach was a legitimate workaround for editor-asset bootstrapping, but it has served its purpose: PR-2 proposes moving the mapping table to an editor asset (needs MCP), while this audit extracts the tunable px constants to named `constexpr` with doc-comments (mechanical, safe). |
+| **B-5** | Low | `ServoProcessor.cpp:592-593`, `:805` | **Avoidance self-radius and teammate-touch radius re-type the 40 cm body radius** (`0.4f` passed as a raw argument; `FMath::Square(2.f * 0.4f)`). Same fix family as B-1: one named constant. Also `ChargeProcessor.cpp:107`'s `DistanceTraveled > 1.f` (charge minimum run-up, meters) is an unnamed behavior threshold -- name it (`MinChargeRunUpM`) beside the other charge constants in `FMeleeMathConstants`. |
+| **B-6** | Low | `ServoProcessor.cpp:241-243` | **`FindConsoleVariable` called per Execute for `Battle.Movement.ProgressiveFallIn`** (twice per call!) while every other cvar in the same function uses a module-scope `TAutoConsoleVariable` or a cached pointer -- including the `WalkSpeedCvar` cached-pointer pattern three lines above. Convert to the same cached-pointer pattern (the file already establishes it). |
+| **B-7** | Low | `DemoPlayerController.cpp:617, 694` + header | **`ClampToGrid` lambda duplicated as a member function**: `OnRMBReleased` defines a local `ClampToGrid` lambda with the magic `12500.f` while `ClampToGridHelper` (added later for the attack path) duplicates it exactly. Delete the lambda; call the member (it's one line). The `12500.f` limit itself is the movement tile half-width minus margin -- belongs beside the plugin's grid constants (deferred to PR-3; the grid extent lives in `BattleCostGrid`, plugin-side, and the demo shouldn't re-derive it). |
+| **B-8** | Low | `DemoPlayerController.cpp:363, 457` vs `DemoHUD` | **Body-center projection height `100.f` (cm) typed at each call site** for screen-projected picking/marquee. One named constant (`BodyLabelHeightCm`) in `DemoTypes.h` next to `FDemoUnitBodyInfo` -- the value is a render hack, and it should be findable when someone tunes the camera. |
+
